{# Footnotes disabled: supplementary facts. Mode-specific questions are inline; only cross-entry questions are included. #}

{% if kind == 'closed_predecessor_frame' %}
# Instructions

Judge only checks.{{ label }}. Is the LEFT selected_predecessor already a complete grammatical frame, so the target does not supply any obligatory missing clause or complement to it?

Locate the exact left source in local_raw_evidence by page_index and source_order. Evaluate what that LEFT text requires, not whether the target looks like a complete sentence. A standalone description, screenplay shot or displayed variable-definition row may start its own paragraph after a complete left frame. Shared topic or the conjunction and does not itself create an unfinished left construction.

Do not certify a left conditional still requiring its main clause, an incomplete verb/preposition or missing obligatory complement. A semicolon, period or formula alone does not close an actual unfinished surrounding frame. Do not invent missing OCR text or an earlier convenient predecessor. If the left frame is not demonstrably closed, answer FALSE.

# True

The exact left frame is closed; the target does not complete an obligatory construction.

# False

An unfinished left construction remains or closure cannot be established.

{% elif kind == 'closed_predecessor_main_reading' %}
# Instructions

Judge only checks.{{ role }}. Is the exact selected_predecessor genuinely in the main reading stream, rather than a detached footnote, caption or publisher apparatus that happens to have been misclassified?

Read its complete raw source and all local sources on that page, including neighboring note markers, smaller-print regions and continuations. A footnote can have several paragraphs, quotations and a long unmarked final block. Do not accept the last supplied paragraph as main reading merely because it is grammatical. Conversely autonomous commentary sections and ordinary narrative are main reading. Require positive role evidence; ambiguity means FALSE.

This question only verifies whether this particular source can certify a new main-paragraph break. It does not select or invent a different, earlier predecessor.

# True

The exact source is genuine main reading.

# False

It belongs to an ancillary stream, or its main-reading role is not established.

{% elif kind == 'conditional_command_required' %}
# Instructions

Judge only checks.{{ key }}. Use the exact selected_predecessor and current target in local_raw_evidence.

Does the target command supply the actual required main instruction governed by the unfinished If-condition ending in a comma in selected_predecessor, so the proposed break cuts one grammatical exercise instruction?

Confirm the literal numbered condition and its immediate command, not merely a shared mathematical topic. A completed example followed by a new problem, independently displayed alternative transformation rows, or an intervening standalone formula cannot establish this fault. Do not invent omitted words or impose paragraph-style preferences. If the target is a distinct item or that grammatical dependency is not established, answer FALSE.

# True

This specific literal relationship is established.

# False

That specific relationship is not established.

{% elif kind == 'nonreading_page_trace' %}
# Instructions

Judge only checks.{{ key }}. Is this retained punctuation-only source a non-reading scan/OCR trace on a page without textual reading content, rather than an intentional reading unit?

Use all raw target layouts, literal source text and its normalized box. A tiny punctuation trace represented by an almost whole-page OCR text rectangle, on an otherwise text-empty page, can be noise; it must not be joined to main prose on a neighboring page. The large rectangle is not evidence of a large printed character.

Punctuation alone does not prove noise. Preserve actual dialogue punctuation, intentional poem/section separators, standalone mathematical notation and signs that have a concrete reading role. Do not invent image pixels or absent text. Require positive evidence of a non-reading artifact; ambiguity means FALSE.

# True

The source is a non-reading page trace kept as prose.

# False

That artifact role is not established.

{% elif kind == 'one_numbered_instruction' %}
# Instructions

Judge only checks.{{ key }}. Use the exact selected_predecessor and current target in local_raw_evidence.

Do the selected predecessor condition and the target command belong to ONE explicitly numbered exercise instruction, with no new numbered item, heading or independent task starting at the target?

Confirm the literal numbered condition and its immediate command, not merely a shared mathematical topic. A completed example followed by a new problem, independently displayed alternative transformation rows, or an intervening standalone formula cannot establish this fault. Do not invent omitted words or impose paragraph-style preferences. If the target is a distinct item or that grammatical dependency is not established, answer FALSE.

# True

This specific literal relationship is established.

# False

That specific relationship is not established.

{% elif kind == 'prior_illustration_unit' %}
# Instructions

Audit ONLY state.checks.{{ key }}. The selected_predecessor identifies the concrete source that this proposed incoming JOIN attaches to. Judge that source and current target, not some older passage chosen because its wording fits.

Is the selected predecessor printed as a separate text band associated with the large nearby illustration, such as a caption or credited literary excerpt, instead of being a normal running-text paragraph?

measured_adjacency reports literal box measurements, not a role verdict. A literary passage can be an image caption even when its wording is ordinary narrative copied from the book. If a narrow text strip directly below the large image ends with a photo credit, evaluate the function of the WHOLE strip; do not label its prose ordinary narrative merely because the same story occurs elsewhere. Conversely a nearby small illustration or a personal name alone cannot establish a separate caption unit.

Use complete local_raw_evidence, the selected source text and the supplied image positions. Image presence alone, a credit name alone, repeated wording alone, or related subject matter is not proof. A passage quoted from ordinary prose can be a separate printed unit with an illustration. Conversely running prose can flow past an image, and captions can continue across pages. Do not infer missing print, unprovided indentation or a fourth page. Ambiguous evidence means FALSE.

# True

The selected source has a concrete independent illustration-associated role.

# False

That independent illustration role is not established.

{% elif kind == 'publisher_apparatus' %}
# Instructions

Judge only checks.{{ key }}. Is this exact source a printed page number, running header/footer or repeated publisher/navigation label, incorrectly kept in the main reading stream?

Use its literal text, page-edge geometry and the surrounding original sources. A combined page number plus chapter/book label at the outer margin can be one apparatus block, including on an otherwise blank page. An empty page does not turn its footer into body text.

Require positive evidence of a publisher/navigation role. Page-edge position, short length, digits or a personal name alone are insufficient. Preserve real chapter openings, section headings, author attributions, dialogue, numbered exercises, endnotes/footnotes and short narrative paragraphs. A heading may start low on a page and remain real content. Do not infer missing text or a fourth page. Ambiguous evidence means FALSE.

# True

The exact source is page apparatus, not main reading content.

# False

A publisher/navigation role is not established.

{% elif kind == 'same_example_setup' %}
# Instructions

Assess only checks.{{ key }}. Does the actual selected predecessor introduce ONE mathematical example whose immediate specification is the target opening, so keeping them in one paragraph is permissible?

A short example introduction and its immediately following Let/Suppose specification may remain in one paragraph even if the introduction ends with a period. Do not require a forced split merely because both pieces have punctuation. Require a direct introduction-to-specification relation, not just related mathematics. A new numbered example, independent exercise, heading or change of task cannot use this proof. Use the actual nearest predecessor, not a more distant convenient one. No unprovided indentation or image text may be invented.

# True

This existing join keeps one directly introduced example and its specification together.

# False

That direct same-example relationship is not established.

{% elif kind == 'target_leaves_illustration' %}
# Instructions

Audit ONLY state.checks.{{ key }}. The selected_predecessor identifies the concrete source that this proposed incoming JOIN attaches to. Judge that source and current target, not some older passage chosen because its wording fits.

Is the CURRENT target source ordinary main reading on its page, rather than part of an illustration caption or detached image/excerpt strip? Judge only its printed role, not whether it starts a complete word or sentence. A main prose fragment can continue earlier body text that is outside this three-page window; you need not find or invent that earlier body text to establish the target role. Related or repeated story wording does not make two sources the same printed unit.

measured_adjacency reports literal box measurements, not a role verdict. A literary passage can be an image caption even when its wording is ordinary narrative copied from the book. If a narrow text strip directly below the large image ends with a photo credit, evaluate the function of the WHOLE strip; do not label its prose ordinary narrative merely because the same story occurs elsewhere. Conversely a nearby small illustration or a personal name alone cannot establish a separate caption unit.

Use complete local_raw_evidence, the selected source text and the supplied image positions. Image presence alone, a credit name alone, repeated wording alone, or related subject matter is not proof. A passage quoted from ordinary prose can be a separate printed unit with an illustration. Conversely running prose can flow past an image, and captions can continue across pages. Do not infer missing print, unprovided indentation or a fourth page. Ambiguous evidence means FALSE.

# True

The target source belongs to ordinary main reading, not to the predecessor illustration caption/excerpt stream.

# False

The main-reading role outside the illustration caption/excerpt stream is not established.

{% elif kind == 'attribution' %}
# Instructions

Assess the entire target source at checks.{{ name }}. Is this block an author/editor/translator attribution or signature attached to a nearby main reading section, rather than page furniture or an explanatory footnote?

Use complete local_raw_evidence for the target, the neighboring section headings and text, and placement. A standalone signature after the end of an entry, article, foreword or its bibliography can be main reading content. Personal initials can serve as such a signature; do not require their expanded name.

The whole block must function as the attribution. Merely mentioning an author inside a long footnote, listing citations, or ending a mixed body/photograph-caption block with a credit is not sufficient. Do not certify a mixed block as an attribution.

Page numbers, chapter folios, repeated running headers and isolated variables are not authorial signatures. Capital letters or periods alone do not prove authorship; establish the block function from its local section placement. An ancillary photo credit is not an author signature for a main reading section.

No prior model score is provided. Do not evaluate paragraph joins, word spelling, or whether the section should be reformatted. If a main-reading attribution cannot be established, answer FALSE.

# True

The complete source functions as an author/editor/translator attribution or signature for a nearby main reading section.

# False

That whole-source main-reading attribution function is not established; it may be page furniture, a footnote, mixed text or another kind of content.

{% elif kind == 'different_dialogue_speaker' %}
# Instructions

For the target at checks.{{ name }}, is its explicitly attributed speaker a DIFFERENT participant from the speaker of the genuine immediately preceding spoken turn?

Use the full three-page local_raw_evidence and preceding_source_index to identify the genuine preceding MAIN-TEXT utterance. Ownership and immediate_paragraph_hint are fallible locators, not a fixed edge. Exclude ancillary material and do not invent participants or text beyond the supplied pages.

Ordinary quotation marks can enclose terms, titles or cited passages rather than dialogue. Do not certify a spoken turn from quotation marks, a new sentence, a conjunction or topical coherence alone.

Resolve each speaker from actual reporting words and nearby dialogue context. A question addressed to another participant and that participant's expressly attributed reply can establish different speakers. A continued utterance by the same person, a new name merely mentioned in dialogue, or a quoted passage from an external source does not establish a speaker change.

Do not infer a different speaker solely from different pronouns, quotation marks, an odd opening or a change of subject. If either identity or the actual turn sequence cannot be established from the supplied neighborhood, answer FALSE. This is a participant-identity question only, not a typesetting judgment.

# True

The actual preceding and target spoken turns are attributed to distinct participants.

# False

A change between identifiable speakers is not established; this may be the same speaker, a non-dialogue quotation or missing context.

{% elif kind == 'fresh_spoken_turn' %}
# Instructions

Does the target text at checks.{{ name }} BEGIN a fresh spoken turn, with its own speaker attribution before or within its utterance, rather than finish the preceding utterance or its reporting clause?

Use the full three-page local_raw_evidence and preceding_source_index to identify the genuine preceding MAIN-TEXT utterance. Ownership and immediate_paragraph_hint are fallible locators, not a fixed edge. Exclude ancillary material and do not invent participants or text beyond the supplied pages.

Ordinary quotation marks can enclose terms, titles or cited passages rather than dialogue. Do not certify a spoken turn from quotation marks, a new sentence, a conjunction or topical coherence alone.

A narrative introduction saying a participant answers, replies or speaks, followed by that participant's new utterance, can begin a fresh turn. A reporting clause placed AFTER a preceding quote can instead belong to that preceding utterance and does not establish a new turn.

This question asks whether a fresh turn starts at this boundary, not whether its speaker differs from the previous speaker. Do not require a split merely for narration after a quote. If no actual new attributed turn is established, answer FALSE.

# True

This target begins a new attributed spoken turn, rather than completing the previous utterance/reporting clause.

# False

A fresh attributed turn at this boundary is not established.

{% elif kind == 'joined_word_complete' %}
# Instructions

For the existing JOIN at checks.{{ name }}, does the target opening supply the literal missing ending of a word that starts at the END of the genuine preceding main-text block?

Use full local_raw_evidence and preceding_source_index. Find the actual nearest main-text predecessor; hints and ownership proposals are fallible. Do not skip an intervening genuine main paragraph, invent missing characters, or use a following page as a predecessor.

A word can be split at a page edge. In alphabetic scripts this can be a prefix and suffix, with normal line-end hyphenation. In Chinese and other unspaced scripts a single lexical expression can also span the edge. Require an actual contiguous word completion at the immediate seam, not merely related meanings or the possibility of placing two complete words next to each other.

Judge the literal word seam before any subsequent punctuation or parenthetical author/editor comments. A comment after the completed word does not undo that word completion. This does not approve the comment's semantics or change its ownership.

A heading, new dialogue turn, independent caption/excerpt or a new numbered item must not be certified just because its first characters could accidentally form a compound with earlier text. If the preceding actual source is not the required lexical stem, or the word completion cannot be established, answer FALSE.

This narrow proof applies only to this incoming join. It does not approve other sources, other boundaries or OCR transcription quality.

# True

The actual adjacent main-text pieces form one word across this boundary; the existing join preserves that word.

# False

Such a specific word completion is not established; ordinary semantic continuity or accidental concatenation is insufficient.

